<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Zuhab</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-safavid-war-1623-1639">Ottoman–Safavid War (1623–1639)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Zuhab was the peace agreement of 1639 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. It ended a long series of wars and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in Ottoman hands. Accounts of the Safavid Empire note that the frontier it established approximates the modern border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and Iraq.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab.jpg" alt="Treaty of Zuhab" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flag of Ottoman Empire (1517-1793) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ottoman_Empire_(1517-1793).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Treaty of Zuhab is also called the Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin. It was signed on 17 May 1639 at Qasr-e Shirin in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and ended the Ottoman-Safavid War of 1623 to 1639. That war was the last conflict in almost 150 years of intermittent wars between the two states over territorial disputes. The treaty can roughly be seen as a confirmation of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> of 1555, and according to Professor Ernest Tucker it was the culmination of a process of normalisation between the two empires that had begun with that peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty confirmed a permanent partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> between the two powers. East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and Shirvan stayed under Safavid control, while western Georgia and most of Western Armenia came fully under Ottoman rule. All of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and became Ottoman Iraq. Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> (Meskheti), which had been Safavid, passed to the Ottomans too, so that Samtskhe was entirely an Ottoman possession. Eastern Armenia remained for more than eight decades under Safavid rule, which divided it into the Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty was vague, and that opened the regions it settled to later contestation. Border disputes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> did not end: between 1555 and 1918 the two signed no fewer than 18 treaties that re-addressed their disputed borders.</p><p>Efforts to fix the exact line, using this treaty and other sources, began in the 19th century and laid out the rough outline of the frontier between modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, which was the Ottoman-Iranian border until 1918. Unlike any other Ottoman-Safavid treaty, Zuhab proved resilient and became a point of departure for almost all later diplomatic agreements between the two neighbours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Zuhab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The most durable was that of the <b>Treaty of Zuhab</b> of 1639, whose line broadly anticipates the modern Iran-Iraq border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
